    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Genome wide association studies (GWAS) have uncovered a number of SNPs associated with a range of diseases including coronary artery disease (CAD). We focus on one particular variation in the SMAD3 gene (rs17293632) that is a key factor in the pathophysiology of CAD. The purpose of this research is to investigate how allelic variation affects gene expression at the SMAD3 gene within 15q22.33, provide validation for the causality of coronary artery associated variant rs17293632, and explore how SMAD3 expression looks in developing cells of mouse models. This called for 1) quantitative investigation of allelic variation at rs17293632 using a dual-luciferase assay and eQTL analysis, 2) validation of causality of rs17293632 through genome editing using a CRISPR/Cas9 system followed by SMAD3 TaqMan qPCR expression analysis, and 3) validation of SMAD3 enhancer in vivo through generation of SMAD3 specific transgenic mice followed by X-gal staining of LacZ reporters. Using these methods we found the SMAD3 expression has allelic dosage dependence at rs17293632, with higher expression of SMAD3 in cells correlated with the number of C alleles at this loci. Through genome editing we confirmed that this variation altered SMAD3 expression in cultured HEK cells. Lastly, in vivo data showed that transgenic lines of C/T mice display different patterns of SMAD3 expression during embryonic development in different tissues. These results indicate the importance and strong causal relationship between allelic variation at rs17293632 and the expression of our CAD relevant gene. In vivo analysis then suggests that this expression is regulated by a wide range of tissue-specific mechanisms that, through further research, may be uncovered. Overall, the data presented here demonstrates the great potential of variant investigation in understanding biological nuances on the smallest scale. These biological insights in turn provide the foundation for the exploration of related genes and the potential development of gene-targeted therapies.",
